      "content": "You're right that the concentrated job losses in Rust Belt communities suggest a distinctly negative outcome for many workers who relied entirely on tariff-sensitive manufacturing. Proponents of this view argue that the reduction in labor standards and wage protections within the US effectively redirected job mobility globally while suppressing domestic pay growth, which mathematically reduces the aggregate benefit for the working class. However, economists often push back by pointing to significant gains in export sectors and electronics manufacturing that specific TPP provisions helped secure, creating a contentious debate about whether you average national outcomes or focus strictly on the suffering in trade-dependent heartland towns.",
